<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waikīkī</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Waikiki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Waikīkī is a beachfront neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> on the south shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. Once a wetland of taro fields and fishponds, it was a residence of Oʻahu's chiefs and the landing place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>'s invasion fleet in 1795. In the 20th century, after its wetlands were drained by the Ala Wai Canal, it became one of the world's best-known beach resorts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/waikiki.jpg" alt="Waikīkī" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pickle the cool · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Waikiki_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
